{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>Pharaoh was angry with his servants.</b> In my view Pharaoh is not a proper noun but an epithet, and it is from the Egyptian language, like Hiram of Tyre; also today every great king among the Ishmaelites is called Nagid of the Faithful. Therefore we find Pharaoh in the days of Abraham and in the days of Joseph, and \"Pharaoh died, and Pharaoh arose.\" So too in the days of Jeremiah — do you not see that Scripture interprets the meaning of Pharaoh, for he is the king of Egypt, and the proof is HaPhra, which is a proper noun; and likewise \"Pharaoh Necho\" (Jeremiah 46:2), for Necho is a proper noun. And do not find it difficult, regarding the Midrash of Solomon's throne, that the king of Egypt did not take it — and so on. The second point: that the chief cupbearer said \"Pharaoh was angry,\" and so too Joseph said, \"Elohim will answer the peace of Pharaoh\" (Genesis 41:16), \"show Pharaoh\" (Genesis 41:28), \"let Pharaoh do\" (Genesis 41:34) — and it is not the way of propriety to mention a proper noun with kings." "<b>Me and the chief baker.</b> He said אֹתִי a second time, for so is the way of speech, as in \"and I, where am I going\" (Genesis 37:30)."], :book :genesis, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 41, :notes {0 "Resolved 'דרש כסא שלמה' as a specific midrashic tradition; 'ועד שני' parsed as introducing a second argument, though the syntax is compressed and the reading uncertain."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"] ["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:47:42.055498055Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}